::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 9x + 4, what is the variable?
{
=A. x#The variable is x because it represents a quantity that can change.
~B. 9#This is the coefficient, not the variable. The variable is the letter that represents a quantity.
~C. 4#This is the constant because it stands alone.
~D. 9x#This is a term, not just the variable.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 9x + 4, what is the coefficient?
{
~A. x#This is the variable. The coefficient is the number attached to the variable.
~B. 4#This is the constant because it stands alone.
=C. 9#The coefficient is the number multiplied by the variable x.
~D. 9x#This is the full variable term. The coefficient is only the numerical factor.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 9x + 4, what is the constant?
{
~A. 9#This is the coefficient. It is multiplied by x.
=B. 4#The 4 stands alone, so it is the constant.
~C. x#This is the variable. A constant is a number that stands alone.
~D. 9x#This is a variable term. It includes the coefficient and the variable.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which are the terms in 6n - 5?
{
~A. 6 and n#These are factors in the variable term, not the terms of the full expression.
~B. n and 5#This misses the coefficient attached to n. The variable term is 6n.
~C. 6 and 5#This identifies numbers but not the variable term.
=D. 6n and 5#The expression has the terms 6n and 5.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression has a coefficient of 12?
{
=A. 12x + 5#The 12 is multiplied by x, so it is the coefficient.
~B. x + 12#The 12 is a constant because it stands alone.
~C. 5x + 12#The coefficient is 5. The 12 is the constant.
~D. x - 12#The 12 is a constant being subtracted, not a coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression has a constant of 7?
{
~A. 7x + 2#The 7 is a coefficient because it is multiplied by x.
=B. 4x + 7#The 7 stands alone, so it is the constant.
~C. x + 2#The constant is 2, not 7.
~D. 7(x + 2)#The 7 is a factor outside the grouped quantity, not a standalone constant.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
A student earns $15 per hour. In the expression 15h, what does h represent?
{
~A. the hourly pay#This describes the coefficient 15, not the variable h.
~B. the total pay#The whole expression 15h represents total pay.
~C. the fixed fee#There is no fixed fee shown in 15h.
=D. the number of hours worked#The coefficient 15 is the hourly pay, so h represents hours worked.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
A student earns $15 per hour. In the expression 15h, what does 15 represent?
{
~A. the number of hours#The variable h represents the number of hours.
~B. the total pay#The expression 15h represents total pay, not the coefficient alone.
~C. a one-time fee#A one-time fee would be a constant added to the expression.
=D. the hourly pay rate#The 15 is the coefficient and represents dollars per hour.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
A rental company charges $25 per day plus a $40 fee. Which expression represents the total cost for d days?
{
=A. 25d + 40#25d represents the daily cost, and 40 represents the fixed fee.
~B. 25 + 40d#This makes 40 depend on the number of days. The daily charge is $25.
~C. 25d#This leaves out the fixed $40 fee.
~D. 40d + 25#This reverses the daily charge and fixed fee.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In 25d + 40, what does 40 represent if a rental company charges $25 per day plus a $40 fee?
{
~A. the number of days#The variable d represents the number of days.
~B. the daily charge#The coefficient 25 represents the daily charge.
~C. the total cost#The full expression 25d + 40 represents the total cost.
=D. the fixed fee#The 40 stands alone, so it represents the fixed fee.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In 3(x + 5), which part is the grouped quantity?
{
~A. 3#This is a factor outside the parentheses.
=B. x + 5#The parentheses group x + 5 as one quantity.
~C. x#This is only one part of the grouped quantity.
~D. 5#This is only one part of the grouped quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
What does 3(x + 5) mean structurally?
{
~A. 3 plus the quantity x + 5#The 3 is written next to the parentheses, which means multiplication.
~B. x plus 15 only#This multiplies only 5 by 3 and ignores that x + 5 is grouped.
=C. 3 groups of the quantity x + 5#The expression means 3 multiplied by the grouped quantity x + 5.
~D. 3x plus 5 only#This multiplies only x by 3 and ignores the grouped quantity.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression has one term at the outer level?
{
=A. 5(x + 2)#At the outer level, this expression is one product.
~B. 4x + 1#This has two terms separated by addition.
~C. 7n - 3#This has two terms separated by subtraction.
~D. 9a + 4#This has two terms separated by addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression has two terms at the outer level?
{
~A. 6(x + 1)#This is one product at the outer level.
~B. 8p#This is one term.
=C. 4m + 9#The terms are 4m and 9.
~D. 2(a + 5)#This is one product at the outer level.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the equation 10s + 30 = 150, what could 10s represent if s is the number of shirts and each shirt costs $10?
{
~A. the fixed fee#The fixed fee would be represented by the standalone 30.
~B. the total cost#The full left side 10s + 30 represents the total before comparing to 150.
~C. the number of shirts#The variable s represents the number of shirts.
=D. the cost of the shirts#10s means $10 times the number of shirts.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the equation 10s + 30 = 150, what could 150 represent?
{
~A. the cost per shirt#The cost per shirt is represented by 10.
=B. the total amount#In this context, 150 can represent the total amount.
~C. the number of shirts#The number of shirts is represented by s.
~D. the fixed fee#The fixed fee is represented by 30.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
A student says the coefficient in 8x + 3 is 3. Which correction is best?
{
=A. The coefficient is 8 because it is multiplied by x.#The coefficient is the number attached to the variable term.
~B. The coefficient is x because x changes.#This identifies the variable. The coefficient is the numerical factor.
~C. The coefficient is 8x because it is a term.#This identifies the full term. The coefficient is only the number factor.
~D. The coefficient is 11 because 8 + 3 \= 11.#This adds parts of the expression instead of identifying the coefficient.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
A student says 6p means 6 + p. Which correction is best?
{
~A. 6p means 6 - p.#This changes the operation incorrectly. A number next to a variable means multiplication.
=B. 6p means 6 multiplied by p.#A coefficient next to a variable means multiplication.
~C. 6p means p divided by 6.#This reverses the meaning. The notation shows multiplication, not division.
~D. 6p means p is always 6.#This confuses a variable expression with a variable value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which statement correctly describes 2b + 9?
{
~A. b is the coefficient.#b is the variable. The coefficient is the number attached to b.
~B. 9 is the coefficient.#9 is the constant because it stands alone.
=C. 2 is the coefficient and 9 is the constant.#The 2 is multiplied by b, and 9 stands alone.
~D. 2b and 9 are factors.#2b and 9 are terms at the outer level, not factors of one product.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which statement correctly describes 7(y + 4)?
{
~A. y + 4 is a constant.#The expression y + 4 includes a variable, so it is not a constant.
~B. 7 is the only term inside the parentheses.#The 7 is outside the parentheses.
=C. y + 4 is a grouped quantity multiplied by 7.#The parentheses group y + 4 as one quantity.
~D. y and 4 cannot be viewed together.#The parentheses show that y + 4 should be viewed together.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 7x + 4, what is the coefficient of x?
{
~A. x#x is the variable, not the coefficient.
=B. 7#The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable, so the coefficient of x is 7.
~C. 4#4 is the constant term because it stands alone.
~D. 11#11 is the sum of 7 and 4, not a part of the expression as written.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression represents 5 more than n?
{
=A. n + 5#The phrase 5 more than n means add 5 to n.
~B. 5n#5n means 5 times n, not 5 more than n.
~C. n - 5#n - 5 means 5 less than n.
~D. 5 - n#5 - n reverses the order and does not mean 5 more than n.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 3a + 2b - 9, what is the constant term?
{
~A. 3a#3a contains a variable, so it is not constant.
~B. 2b#2b contains a variable, so it is not constant.
~C. 5#5 is the sum of two coefficients, not a term in the expression.
=D. -9#The constant term is the term without a variable, including its sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which expression represents twice a number decreased by 6?
{
=A. 2x - 6#Twice a number is 2x, and decreased by 6 means subtract 6.
~B. 2(x - 6)#This means twice the entire quantity x - 6, which is different from decreasing 2x by 6.
~C. x² - 6#x² means x times itself, not twice x.
~D. 6 - 2x#This reverses the subtraction order.
}

::ALG1_U01_L05_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L05_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 4(x + 3), how can x + 3 be viewed?
{
~A. As a constant term.#x + 3 contains a variable, so it is not a constant term.
~B. As the coefficient of 4.#A coefficient multiplies a variable or quantity; x + 3 is not the coefficient of 4.
=C. As one quantity multiplied by 4.#The parentheses show that x + 3 can be treated as one quantity being multiplied by 4.
~D. As an exponent.#There is no exponent shown in x + 3.
}
